@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Cloning, Branching, and Merging";
    ViewData["ActiveMenu"] = "AzureDevOps";
}

<div class="container py-4">
    <p class="text-center lead">Cloning, branching, and merging are essential version control concepts in Azure Repos. These features allow developers to work on code collaboratively while maintaining a structured workflow.</p>
    <hr />

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Cloning a Repository</h2>
        <p>Cloning allows developers to create a local copy of a remote repository, enabling them to work offline and make changes before pushing updates.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="row align-items-center">
        <div class="col-md-6">
            <h4>Step 1: Navigate to Azure Repos</h4>
            <p>Sign in to <a href="https://dev.azure.com/" target="_blank">Azure DevOps</a>, select your project, and go to **Repos**.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6 text-center">
            <img src="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/devops/media/git-clone.png" alt="Cloning a Repository" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Cloning a repository locally</small></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row align-items-center mt-4">
        <div class="col-md-6 text-center">
            <img src="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/devops/media/git-command-clone.png" alt="Git Clone Command" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Running the Git Clone Command</small></p>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6">
            <h4>Step 2: Clone the Repository</h4>
            <p>Copy the repository URL and run the following command in your terminal or Git Bash:</p>
            <pre><code>git clone &lt;repository-url&gt;</code></pre>
            <p>Alternatively, you can clone the repository using **Visual Studio, VS Code, or GitHub Desktop**.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Branching in Azure Repos</h2>
        <p>Branching allows developers to create isolated environments for new features, bug fixes, or experiments. Azure Repos supports multiple branching strategies:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Feature Branching:</strong> Each new feature is developed in a separate branch.</li>
            <li><strong>GitFlow:</strong> A structured workflow with **develop, feature, release, and hotfix branches**.</li>
            <li><strong>Trunk-Based Development:</strong> A single branch (main) with short-lived feature branches.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="row align-items-center">
        <div class="col-md-6">
            <h4>Step 3: Create a New Branch</h4>
            <p>To create a new branch locally, run the following command:</p>
            <pre><code>git checkout -b feature-branch</code></pre>
            <p>Push the new branch to Azure Repos with:</p>
            <pre><code>git push -u origin feature-branch</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6 text-center">
            <img src="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/devops/media/git-branching.png" alt="Creating a Branch" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Creating and switching branches in Git</small></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Merging Branches</h2>
        <p>Merging integrates changes from one branch into another. This can be done through pull requests or using Git commands.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="row align-items-center">
        <div class="col-md-6 text-center">
            <img src="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/devops/media/git-merge.png" alt="Merging Branches" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Merging branches in Azure Repos</small></p>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6">
            <h4>Step 4: Merge a Branch</h4>
            <p>To merge changes from a feature branch into **main**, use:</p>
            <pre><code>git checkout main
            git merge feature-branch</code></pre>
            <p>If conflicts arise, resolve them manually before completing the merge.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Conclusion</h2>
        <p>
            Cloning, branching, and merging are fundamental to managing code in Azure Repos. By following best practices for branching and using pull requests, teams can ensure seamless collaboration and high-quality code integration.
        </p>
        <p>
            The next step is to explore **Pull Requests and Code Reviews** in Azure Repos.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="d-flex justify-content-between mt-4">
        <a href="@Url.Action("CreatingRepositories", "AzureDevOps")" class="btn btn-outline-secondary">&larr; Previous: Creating Repositories</a>
        <a href="@Url.Action("PullRequestsCodeReviews", "AzureDevOps")" class="btn btn-outline-secondary">Next: Pull Requests and Code Reviews &rarr;</a>
    </div>
</div>